        <h2>Timing: When to Use Nicotine for Exercise</h2>

        <h3>Before Workout (20&ndash;30 Minutes Prior)</h3>
        <p>Using a pouch 20&ndash;30 minutes before exercise allows nicotine to absorb and reach peak plasma levels right as you begin. This maximises focus and cognitive benefits during your session. Drawback: if you experience nausea, you've already begun exercising and can't easily remove the pouch.</p>

        <h3>During Exercise</h3>
        <p>Placing a pouch at the start of your session allows gradual absorption during the workout. This can extend alertness and focus through the second half of long sessions (45+ minutes). Drawback: salivation and swallowing during intense cardio is uncomfortable, and some athletes find it distracting.</p>

        <h3>After Workout (Post-Exercise Recovery)</h3>
        <p>Some athletes use nicotine post-workout for focus and mental clarity during cooldown or recovery stretches. This avoids any cardiovascular stress during peak exertion. Drawback: minimal performance benefit if used after exercise is complete.</p>

        <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> For most athletic uses, 20&ndash;30 minutes pre-exercise is optimal. You get peak nicotine levels during your main set without interference from salivation or stomach discomfort.</p>

        <h2>Hydration and Fluid Management</h2>

        <h3>Why Extra Hydration Matters</h3>
        <p>Nicotine increases urine output by roughly 10&ndash;15% through its mild diuretic effect. Combined with sweat loss during exercise, this elevates dehydration risk significantly. Dehydration impairs performance, increases injury risk, and can trigger heat illness.</p>

        <h3>Hydration Protocol for Nicotine + Exercise</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pre-exercise (2 hours before):</strong> Drink 400&ndash;600ml water. If using nicotine, drink an extra 200ml (total 600&ndash;800ml).</li>
          <li><strong>During exercise:</strong> Aim for 150&ndash;250ml water per 15 minutes of exercise. With nicotine, add 50ml extra per 15-minute interval.</li>
          <li><strong>Post-exercise:</strong> Rehydrate with 150% of fluid lost during exercise. Nicotine users should drink even more — aim for 2.3 litres per kg of body weight lost.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Electrolyte consideration:</strong> Heavy sweating + diuretics depletes sodium and potassium. Using an electrolyte drink (not just water) during long sessions (90+ minutes) is especially important for nicotine users.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Is nicotine banned in professional sports?</h3>
        <p>Nicotine itself is not on the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) banned list, so it's legal in most Olympic sports. However, individual sports organisations may have their own rules — check with your sport's governing body. Some sports (e.g., certain Olympic events) monitor nicotine use but allow it. Always verify before competing.</p>

        <h3>Can I use nicotine pouches with pre-workout supplements?</h3>
        <p>Nicotine + caffeine combine as stimulants, elevating heart rate and blood pressure significantly. This increases cardiac stress and nausea risk. If you use pre-workout drinks (which typically contain 200&ndash;400mg caffeine), avoid nicotine or use a very low-strength pouch (3mg). Combining high-strength nicotine with strong pre-workout is risky.</p>

        <h3>Will nicotine help me lose weight?</h3>
        <p>Nicotine slightly increases metabolism and may suppress appetite, leading to minor weight loss (1&ndash;2kg over months). However, addiction and long-term health risks far outweigh this marginal benefit. It's not a weight-loss strategy — it's a stimulant with serious trade-offs.</p>

        <h3>What if I feel dizzy or nauseous during exercise with nicotine?</h3>
        <p>Remove the pouch immediately. Dizziness during exercise indicates nicotine is causing excessive cardiovascular stress. Rest, hydrate, and cool down. Next time, use a lower strength (drop from 8mg to 4mg) or skip nicotine entirely. Your safety is more important than any performance edge.</p>

        <h3>Is nicotine effective for endurance sports like marathons?</h3>
        <p>Research shows modest benefits (1&ndash;3% time-to-exhaustion improvement), but the cardiovascular stress of high-dose nicotine during long endurance efforts could negate gains. For marathon running, if you use nicotine at all, stick to low strength (4&ndash;6mg) and practise with it in training first. Experiment during training, not race day.</p>

        <h3>Can beginners use nicotine pouches for fitness?</h3>
        <p>Not recommended. If you're new to nicotine, your body hasn't developed tolerance. Low-strength pouches (3&ndash;4mg) with moderate exercise are the only safe option if you insist on experimenting. New users are more prone to nausea, dizziness, and adverse effects. Build fitness first, experiment with nicotine later (if at all).</p>

        <h3>Should I use nicotine for strength training or cardio?</h3>
        <p>Nicotine is more beneficial for activities requiring focus and endurance (cardio, tactical sports) than pure strength training. For weight lifting, the focus gain may be marginal, and cardiovascular stress during heavy lifts is a real concern. If you use nicotine for gym sessions, cardio days make more sense than strength-focused days.</p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-primary/10 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-md p-4 my-6">
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground"><strong class="text-primary">Health Warning:</strong> If you have high blood pressure, heart palpitations, arrhythmia, or any cardiovascular condition, do NOT use nicotine pouches during exercise. The combined stress is dangerous. Always consult your doctor before using nicotine if you have any health concerns.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>Nicotine pouches can provide modest performance benefits — improved focus, slight endurance gains, and sharper decision-making — making them appealing to some athletes. The science is real but modest: expect 1&ndash;3% performance improvement at best, not transformational change.</p>

        <p>If you decide to use pouches for training or competition, choose slim formats with moderate strength (4&ndash;8mg) and refreshing flavours. Practise with them during training, not race day. Prioritise hydration aggressively, monitor for nausea or dizziness, and understand the addiction risk.</p>

        <p>For many athletes, the modest performance edge doesn't justify long-term nicotine dependence. Consider whether the gains align with your athletic goals and personal health priorities. And remember: consistency, sleep, nutrition, and training quality matter far more than any ergogenic aid.</p>

